# Commitizen commits

Every git commit message must follow Commitizen / Conventional Commits. Do not use free-form or one-word messages.

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

## Types

Use only: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `chore`, `ci`, `build`, `revert`.

- `feat` — new user-facing behavior
- `fix` — bug fix
- `docs` — docs only
- `style` — formatting, no logic change
- `refactor` — internal change, no behavior change
- `perf` — performance
- `test` — tests only
- `chore` — tooling, deps, repo housekeeping
- `ci` — CI/CD
- `build` — build/packaging
- `revert` — revert a prior commit

## Subject rules

- Imperative, lowercase, no trailing period: `add note schema`, not `Added note schema.`
- One line, ~50 characters; put detail in the body.
- Scope is optional and lowercase (`api`, `notes`, `deps`).

```
# ❌ BAD
update stuff
Added pydantic models.

# ✅ GOOD
feat(notes): add create-note request schema

fix(api): return 404 when note is missing

chore: add ruff and pytest quality gates
```

## Breaking changes

Mark them with `!` after the type/scope and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer:

```
feat(api)!: require NoteCreate body on POST /notes

BREAKING CHANGE: raw JSON dicts are no longer accepted.
```

When the user asks to commit, draft the message in this format and pass it via HEREDOC. Never invent a type that is not in the list above.
